    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpNewMonthBody": "A new month starts empty. Fill it like the month before or from a template, by hand or automatically every month (Budget menu ⋮, “Fill automatically…”).",
  "@helpNewMonthBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "helpNewMonthSteps": "Only empty categories are filled: amounts you already set stay.\nWhat you change by hand always wins, also against your partner’s phone filling the same month.\nUndo right after filling resets only the categories you have not changed since.",
  "@helpNewMonthSteps": {
    "description": "Numbered steps of a help topic, one per line"
  },
  "helpOnBudgetTitle": "In or out of the budget",
  "@helpOnBudgetTitle": {
    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpOnBudgetBody": "Accounts in the budget hold the money you spend and plan with: their balance is what you budget. Accounts outside the budget only count towards net worth.",
  "@helpOnBudgetBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "helpOnBudgetSteps": "In the budget: checking, cash, credit cards and the account your salary arrives in.\nOutside: savings, brokerage, crypto and pension accounts, loans.\nA reserve inside your checking account cannot be outside: budget it into a category like Savings instead.\nTaking an account out offers to release the money you budgeted for it, so To Be Budgeted does not go negative.",
  "@helpOnBudgetSteps": {
    "description": "Numbered steps of a help topic, one per line"
  },
  "helpPersonalTitle": "Personal accounts",
  "@helpPersonalTitle": {
    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpPersonalBody": "For couples who sync: a personal account belongs to you. Your partner sees its name and balance in net worth, but not its transactions, and cannot change it.",
  "@helpPersonalBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "helpPersonalSteps": "Personal accounts are always outside the budget.\nA transfer between a shared account and your personal one syncs as the shared side only.\nAn account can become personal only before it first syncs, so mark it before pairing. Sharing it later is always possible and sends its history.",
  "@helpPersonalSteps": {
    "description": "Numbered steps of a help topic, one per line"
  },
  "helpAccountTypesTitle": "Account types",
  "@helpAccountTypesTitle": {
    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpAccountTypesBody": "The type sets the icon and whether a new account starts in the budget. Savings and Investment accounts start outside the budget; the switch below changes that.",
  "@helpAccountTypesBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "helpAccountsTitle": "Accounts and net worth",
  "@helpAccountsTitle": {
    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpAccountsBody": "Net worth adds up all accounts, in or out of the budget. If you sync with a partner, it is your household’s net worth, and the lines below it split it into shared, yours and your partner’s.",
  "@helpAccountsBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "helpAccountsSteps": "Off budget: counts towards net worth only.\nPersonal: yours; your partner sees only its name and balance.\nPartner’s: your partner’s personal accounts, read-only, with the balance their phone last shared.",
  "@helpAccountsSteps": {
    "description": "Lines of a help topic explaining the account badges, one per line"
  },
  "helpTransferTitle": "Transfers",
  "@helpTransferTitle": {
    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpTransferBody": "A transfer moves money between two of your accounts. It is neither income nor spending.",
  "@helpTransferBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "helpTransferSteps": "Between two budget accounts (checking to cash): no category, nothing changes in the budget.\nFrom a budget account to one outside (checking to brokerage): pick the category you budgeted for it, for example Stocks & ETFs. Without a category the money comes out of To Be Budgeted.\nBack from outside into the budget (savings to checking): without a category it adds to To Be Budgeted.",
  "@helpTransferSteps": {
    "description": "Numbered steps of a help topic, one per line"
  },
  "helpSyncTitle": "Syncing with a partner",
  "@helpSyncTitle": {
    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpSyncBody": "Two phones can share one budget: over your home Wi-Fi without any cloud, or through a Google Drive account you both use when you are apart. Everything is encrypted before it leaves the phone.",
  "@helpSyncBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "helpSyncSteps": "Every change syncs on its own. If both phones change the same thing, the later change wins and the other one is listed under conflicts, where you can keep it.\nPersonal accounts share only their name and balance.\nBoth phones need the same app version.",
  "@helpSyncSteps": {
    "description": "Numbered steps of a help topic, one per line"
  },
  "helpCsvTitle": "Importing a CSV file",
  "@helpCsvTitle": {
    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpCsvBody": "Export your transactions from your bank as a CSV file. LedgerZero matches the columns by their names, and you can correct the matching before importing.",
  "@helpCsvBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "helpCsvSteps": "The first row must hold the column names: delete any lines above it.\nAmounts must be in one column, negative for money going out. Separate debit and credit columns are not supported yet.\nOn the review screen, untick the rows you do not want.",
  "@helpCsvSteps": {
    "description": "Numbered steps of a help topic, one per line"
  },
  "helpAppLockTitle": "App lock",
  "@helpAppLockTitle": {
    "description": "Help topic title"
  },
  "helpAppLockBody": "The app locks with your fingerprint, face or phone PIN once it has been in the background for the time you choose. Pulling down notifications or answering a system dialog does not lock it, and after unlocking you are back where you were.",
  "@helpAppLockBody": {
    "description": "Help topic text"
  },
  "hintSavingsTransfer": "Budgeted money stays in your checking account until you move it: add a transfer to your savings or brokerage account and pick this category.",
  "@hintSavingsTransfer": {
    "description": "One-time hint in the Savings & Investments group once money is budgeted there"
  },
  "hintOffBudgetAccount": "Account created. It is outside the budget: to save into it, budget the amount in Savings & Investments and add a transfer with that category.",
  "@hintOffBudgetAccount": {
    "description": "One-time snack bar after creating the first account outside the budget"
  },
  "hintPartnerAccounts": "These are your partner’s personal accounts. You see their balance; their transactions stay on your partner’s phone.",
  "@hintPartnerAccounts": {
    "description": "One-time hint above the partner's accounts in the account list"
  },
  "guideTransfersTitle": "Transfers",
  "@guideTransfersTitle": {
    "description": "User guide chapter title"
  },
  "guideTransfersBody": "A transfer moves money between two of your accounts and is neither income nor spending. Between two budget accounts it needs no category and changes nothing in the budget. From a budget account to an account outside the budget, the money leaves the budget: pick the category you budgeted for it, or leave the category empty to take it from To Be Budgeted. Money coming back from outside without a category adds to To Be Budgeted.",
  "@guideTransfersBody": {
    "description": "User guide chapter text"
  },
  "guideSavingTitle": "Saving and investing",
  "@guideSavingTitle": {
    "description": "User guide chapter title"
  },
  "guideSavingBody": "Keep savings and investments in their own accounts outside the budget, so price changes and interest never disturb it. Each month, budget what you want to put aside in Savings & Investments (Savings, Stocks & ETFs, Crypto, Bonds, Retirement). When you move the money, add a transfer from your budget account to the savings account and pick that category: the category is spent, the savings account grows and To Be Budgeted stays the same. Example: budget 500 in Stocks & ETFs, then transfer 500 from checking to your brokerage account with Stocks & ETFs. A reserve you keep in your checking account stays in a category like Savings instead, where it carries over every month.",
  "@guideSavingBody": {
    "description": "User guide chapter text"
  },
  "guidePartnerTitle": "Using LedgerZero with a partner",
  "@guidePartnerTitle": {
    "description": "User guide chapter title"
  },
  "guidePartnerBody": "Two phones can share one budget. Pair them in Sync Settings: over your home Wi-Fi (scan the QR code on the other phone; both need LedgerZero open on the same Wi-Fi) or through a Google Drive account you both use. Every change syncs on its own; if you both change the same thing, the later change wins and Sync Settings lists the other one. Accounts you mark as personal stay yours: your partner sees their name and balance in net worth, but not their transactions. Mark an account as personal before the first sync; you can still share it later.",
  "@guidePartnerBody": {
    "description": "User guide chapter text"
  },
  "guideSecurityTitle": "Security and backups",
  "@guideSecurityTitle": {
    "description": "User guide chapter title"
  },
  "guideSecurityBody": "Your data is stored encrypted on your phone, and the key is kept in the phone’s secure hardware. The app lock asks for your fingerprint, face or phone PIN after the app was in the background. Create encrypted backups regularly in Settings > Backup & Export and keep the backup password safe: without it a backup cannot be restored.",
  "@guideSecurityBody": {
    "description": "User guide chapter text"
  },
  "guideImportTitle": "Importing",
  "@guideImportTitle": {
    "description": "User guide chapter title"
  },
  "guideImportBody": "Settings > Import CSV reads your bank’s CSV export: the first row must hold the column names, and amounts must be in one column (negative for money going out). Columns are matched by name and can be corrected before importing. YNAB budgets can be imported from Settings as well.",
  "@guideImportBody": {
    "description": "User guide chapter text"
  },
  "faqLockQ": "What if my fingerprint is not recognised?",
  "@faqLockQ": {
    "description": "FAQ question"
  },
  "faqLockA": "The app lock also accepts your phone’s PIN, pattern or password, the same way your phone’s lock screen does.",
  "@faqLockA": {
    "description": "FAQ answer"
  },
  "faqSavingQ": "How do I put money into savings or investments?",
  "@faqSavingQ": {
    "description": "FAQ question"
  },
  "faqSavingA": "Keep the savings or brokerage account outside the budget. Each month, budget the amount in a Savings & Investments category, then add a transfer from your checking account to the savings account and pick that category. The category is spent and To Be Budgeted stays the same.",
  "@faqSavingA": {
    "description": "FAQ answer"
  },
  "faqPersonalQ": "Can I keep an account to myself?",
  "@faqPersonalQ": {
    "description": "FAQ question"
  },
  "faqPersonalA": "Yes. Switch on “Personal” when you create the account. Your partner then sees only its name and balance; its transactions stay on your phone. Do this before the first sync: an account that has already synced stays shared.",
  "@faqPersonalA": {
    "description": "FAQ answer"
  },
